# AXUM Rover — Cursor Rules
# ============================================================
# Read this entire file before suggesting any code.
# Every decision here was made deliberately — do not change
# architectural choices without flagging them explicitly.

## WHAT THIS PROJECT IS

AXUM is an autonomous museum-grade artefact triage and conservation
robot built for WRO 2026 Future Innovators (theme: "Robots Meet Culture").
It picks up cultural artefacts, scans them with multi-modal sensing,
physically intervenes to preserve them, and produces a searchable catalogue.

Competition deadline: mid-August 2026 (national selection, Ethiopia).
Team size: 2 people. Build starts: early June 2026.

---

## HARDWARE — READ BEFORE TOUCHING ANY SERIAL/PIN CODE

### Main compute
- Arduino Mega 2560 — main MCU, all hardware control
- ESP32-CAM (AI Thinker) — camera only, WiFi stream + trigger capture
- Laptop (Windows 11, i7 13th Gen, 8GB RAM, no discrete GPU) — all AI/CV
- STM32F411 Black Pill — co-processor for 12-bit ADC via I2C

### Motors & motion
- Drive: JGA25-370 DC gearmotor 12V 1:50 ×4, via L298N ×2
  - Left:  ENA=44, IN1=7,  IN2=8
  - Right: ENA=45, IN1=12, IN2=13
  - Encoders: interrupt pins 18 (left), 19 (right)
- Arm: EEZYbotARM MK2 style, 4-DOF + gripper
  - S0 base=pin3(MG996R), S1 shoulder=pin4(MG996R)
  - S2 elbow=pin6(MG90S), S3 wrist=pin9(SG90), S4 gripper=pin10(SG90)
  - PCA9685 PWM driver handles all servos (eliminates jitter)
- Turntable: 28BYJ-48 stepper + ULN2003, pins 22/23/24/25
  - 2048 steps/rev (half-step), 36 photos at 10° increments

### Camera & lighting
- ESP32-CAM stream:   http://<IP>:81/stream
- ESP32-CAM capture: http://<IP>/capture
- Trigger pin: Arduino 30 → ESP32 GPIO 13
- LED ring: WS2812B NeoPixel 12-LED, pin 11
  - Split into 4 quadrants (N/E/S/W) for photometric stereo
  - Each quadrant: 3 LEDs via IRF520N MOSFET
  - Quadrant pins: 33(N), 34(E), 35(S), 36(W)

### Sensors
- HC-SR04 ultrasonic: front (trig=26, echo=27), side (trig=28, echo=29)
- E18-D80NK IR proximity ×3: tray occupancy + arm home detection
- Load cell 500g + HX711: turntable mass measurement
- FSR 402 ×2: one per gripper finger (grip force)
- MPU-6050 IMU: at wrist (mass + vibration)
- Piezoelectric disc: spring-loaded hardness probe on arm tip
- Micro peristaltic pump 5V: consolidant injection
- Solenoid 5V 10mm: acoustic tap tester

### Arm-tip hardware
- FSR 402 ×2 (grip force per finger)
- Micro-brush motor 3V DC (inscription cleaning)
- Micro-syringe 0.3mm needle (consolidant injection via silicone tube)
- Piezoelectric disc probe (hardness mapping)
- RGB LED ×1 (status)
- MPU-6050 IMU (mass + vibration)

### Power
- Rail A: 7.4V 2S LiPo → L298N → L298N 5V out → Arduino Vin
- Rail B: 5V 3A USB power bank → servos + stepper + ESP32-CAM
- NEVER mix servo and motor power rails
- 1000µF 16V caps across each motor driver input
- AMS1117-3.3 dedicated regulator for ESP32-CAM

---

## ARDUINO SERIAL PROTOCOL
# All commands are ASCII strings terminated with newline.
# Python sends → Arduino responds.

PING              → PONG
STOP              → OK:STOP
STATUS            → JSON {front, side, enc_l, enc_r}
DRIVE:<l>,<r>     → OK:DRIVE      (speed -255 to 255)
ARM:<s0>,<s1>,<s2>,<s3> → OK:ARM (angles 0-180)
GRIP:<angle>      → OK:GRIP       (0=closed, 160=open, slow movement)
POSE:<name>       → OK:POSE       (named poses: HOME, PICK, PLACE, SCAN)
STEP:<steps>      → OK:STEP       (turntable stepper)
ROTATE:<degrees>  → OK:ROTATE     (turntable by degrees)
PHOTO             → OK:PHOTO      (trigger ESP32-CAM capture)
LED:<r>,<g>,<b>   → OK:LED
LED:SCAN / LED:READY / LED:ERROR / LED:SUCCESS
LED:QUAD:<N|E|S|W> → OK:LED:QUAD  (fire single NeoPixel quadrant)
LED:OFF           → OK:LED:OFF
TAP               → OK:TAP        (fire solenoid tapper)
INJECT:<ul>       → OK:INJECT     (fire peristaltic pump, microlitres)
PRESSURE          → PRESSURE:<value>
IMPLANT           → OK:IMPLANT    (fire RFID plunger servo)

# IMPORTANT: send_with_retry() in src/arm/controller.py handles
# all serial communication. Never write raw serial.write() calls
# in Python — always go through ArduinoSerial.send_command().

